ref_en:
Assuming n Buffs and m targets, the following modes are supported: 
 0: (Generous) Apply a total of k*m Buffs: For each of the m objects, randomly select k different Buffs from n Buffs to add. k takes the MaxCount parameter. If k is left blank or filled with 0, it means k=n, i.e., give all Buffs to all m objects; 
 1: (Sequential distribution, max 1) Apply a total of min(n,m) Buffs: Distribute n Buffs to m objects in order until the Buffs are exhausted or objects are exhausted; 
 2: (Random distribution, max 1) Apply a total of min(n,m) Buffs: Randomly distribute n Buffs one by one to one of the m objects. Objects cannot be selected repeatedly (random without replacement) until Buffs are exhausted or objects are exhausted; 
 3: (Sequential distribution, until exhausted) Apply a total of n Buffs: Distribute n Buffs to m objects in order. If a round is completed, start from the beginning until Buffs are exhausted; 
 4: (Random distribution, until exhausted) Apply a total of n Buffs: Randomly distribute n Buffs one by one to one of the m objects. Objects can be selected repeatedly (random with replacement) until Buffs are exhausted; 
 5: (Sequential distribution, 1 per person) Apply a total of m Buffs: Distribute n Buffs to m objects in order. If n Buffs are exhausted and it's not enough, start from the beginning until all m objects have Buffs; 
 6: (Random distribution without replacement, 1 per person) Apply a total of m Buffs: Randomly distribute n Buffs to m objects without replacement. If n Buffs are exhausted and it's not enough, start a new round of random distribution until all m objects have Buffs; 
 7: (Random distribution with replacement, 1 per person) Apply a total of m Buffs: Randomly distribute n Buffs to m objects with replacement.

